{"task_id": "HumanEval/24", "solution": "def largest_divisor(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Return the largest integer d < n such that n % d == 0.\n\n    Args:\n        n: integer greater than 1.\n\n    Returns:\n        The largest proper divisor of n (an int). For prime n this is 1.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: if n is not an int.\n        ValueError: if n <= 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an int\")\n    if n <= 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be greater than 1\")\n\n    # Quick handle even numbers: largest proper divisor is n/2\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return n // 2\n\n    # For odd n, search for the smallest divisor > 1 up to sqrt(n).\n    # If we find a divisor i, the complementary divisor n//i is the largest proper divisor.\n    import math\n    limit = math.isqrt(n)\n    for i in range(3, limit + 1, 2):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return n // i\n\n    # No divisor found -> n is prime\n    return 1"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/25", "solution": "def factorize(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Return list of prime factors of given integer in the order from smallest to largest.\n    Each of the factors should be listed number of times corresponding to how many times it appeares in factorization.\n    Input number should be equal to the product of all factors\n    >>> factorize(8)\n    [2, 2, 2]\n    >>> factorize(25)\n    [5, 5]\n    >>> factorize(70)\n    [2, 5, 7]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"n must be an integer\")\n    if n < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be >= 1\")\n\n    if n == 1:\n        return []\n\n    factors: List[int] = []\n\n    # remove factors of 2\n    while n % 2 == 0:\n        factors.append(2)\n        n //= 2\n\n    # check odd divisors up to sqrt(n)\n    d = 3\n    while d * d <= n:\n        while n % d == 0:\n            factors.append(d)\n            n //= d\n        d += 2\n\n    # if remaining n > 1, it's a prime\n    if n > 1:\n        factors.append(n)\n\n    return factors"}
